* CNN Design Decision Guide
** Step 1: Identify Data Type & Dimensionality
*** 1D (Sequences, Time Series)
**** Use Conv1D layers
**** Examples: Audio, Text, Signals
**** Architectures: Temporal CNNs, Wavenet-inspired
*** 2D (Images, Grids)
**** Use Conv2D layers
**** Standard for computer vision
**** Architectures: Most popular ones below
*** 3D (Videos, Volumes)
**** Use Conv3D or 2D+Time (e.g., ConvLSTM)
**** Examples: Medical Imaging, Video Analysis
**** Architectures: C3D, I3D, SlowFast
** Step 2: Define Task & Objectives
*** Classification
**** Single/Multi-label image classification
**** Metrics: Accuracy, Top-k
*** Object Detection/Segmentation
**** Bounding boxes, masks
**** Architectures: YOLO, Faster R-CNN, U-Net, Mask R-CNN
*** Regression
**** Predicting continuous values (e.g., age from image)
**** Metrics: MSE, MAE
*** Generative/Other
**** GANs, Autoencoders, Style Transfer
**** Architectures: DCGAN, U-Net variants
*** Key Objectives
**** High Accuracy vs. Efficiency Trade-off
**** Real-time (low latency) vs. Batch Processing
**** Deployment: Edge devices (lightweight) vs. Servers (complex)
** Step 3: Consider Constraints
*** Computational Resources
**** High: Use deep, parameter-heavy models (e.g., ResNet-152)
**** Low: Lightweight (e.g., MobileNet, EfficientNet)
*** Dataset Size
**** Large: Complex models to avoid underfitting
**** Small: Use transfer learning, data augmentation
*** Interpretability
**** Needed: Simpler models like LeNet or VGG
**** Not: Black-box like Inception
** Step 4: Select Architecture Family
*** Classic/Simple
**** LeNet-5 (1998)
***** Structure: Conv-Pool-Conv-Pool-FC
***** Pros: Simple, for small images (e.g., digits)
***** Cons: Not deep, limited for complex tasks
***** Use: Educational, low-res data
**** AlexNet (2012)
***** Structure: 5 Conv + 3 FC, ReLU, Dropout, Data Aug
***** Pros: Deep for its time, won ImageNet
***** Cons: High params (60M), no bottlenecks
***** Use: Baseline for image classification
*** Depth-Focused
**** VGG (2014)
***** Variants: VGG16/19
***** Structure: Stacked 3x3 Convs, MaxPool, FC head
***** Pros: Uniform, easy to implement, good features
***** Cons: Very deep (up to 19 layers), 138M params, memory-intensive
***** Use: Feature extraction, transfer learning
**** ResNet (2015)
***** Variants: ResNet-18/50/101/152, ResNetV2
***** Structure: Residual blocks with skip connections
***** Pros: Very deep (100+ layers), mitigates vanishing gradients
***** Cons: Higher compute for deeper variants
***** Use: State-of-the-art classification, backbone for detection
*** Width & Multi-Scale
**** Inception (GoogLeNet, 2014)
***** Variants: InceptionV1-V4, Inception-ResNet
***** Structure: Inception modules (parallel 1x1,3x3,5x5 convs + pool)
***** Pros: Efficient params (22M), multi-scale features
***** Cons: Complex to implement, harder to tune
***** Use: When param efficiency matters
**** Xception (2016)
***** Structure: Depthwise separable convs + residuals
***** Pros: Better than InceptionV3 with fewer params
***** Cons: Still complex
***** Use: Mobile/embedded vision
*** Dense Connectivity
**** DenseNet (2017)
***** Variants: DenseNet-121/169/201
***** Structure: Dense blocks (each layer connects to all subsequent)
***** Pros: Feature reuse, fewer params (8M for 121), strong gradients
***** Cons: High memory during training
***** Use: When memory allows, for high accuracy
*** Efficiency-Optimized
**** MobileNet (2017)
***** Variants: V1-V3
***** Structure: Depthwise separable convs, inverted residuals (V2)
***** Pros: Lightweight (4M params), fast on mobile
***** Cons: Lower accuracy than heavier models
***** Use: Edge devices, real-time apps
**** EfficientNet (2019)
***** Variants: B0-B7
***** Structure: Compound scaling (depth/width/resolution), MBConv blocks
***** Pros: SOTA efficiency-accuracy Pareto front
***** Cons: Requires careful scaling
***** Use: Balanced performance, scalable
**** NASNet / AutoML (2018+)
***** Structure: Neural Architecture Search-derived cells
***** Pros: Automated optimization
***** Cons: Hard to reproduce without resources
***** Use: Advanced, when customizing
*** Modern/Transformers Hybrid
**** Vision Transformer (ViT, 2020)
***** Structure: Patch embeddings + Transformer encoder
***** Pros: Scalable, attention-based
***** Cons: Data-hungry, less inductive bias
***** Use: Large datasets, alternative to CNNs
**** ConvNeXt (2022)
***** Structure: Modernized ResNet with ConvNext blocks
***** Pros: Competes with Transformers in efficiency
***** Cons: Still CNN-based
***** Use: Pure CNN in transformer era
** Step 5: Customization & Best Practices
*** Building Blocks
**** Conv Layers: Kernel size (3x3 common), Strides, Padding
**** Activation: ReLU, LeakyReLU, Swish
**** Normalization: BatchNorm, LayerNorm, GroupNorm
**** Pooling: Max/Avg/Global
**** Regularization: Dropout, L1/L2, Augmentation
*** Enhancements
**** Transfer Learning: Pretrain on ImageNet/COCO
**** Ensemble: Combine models for robustness
**** Pruning/Quantization: For deployment
*** Evaluation
**** Metrics: Task-specific (e.g., mAP for detection)
**** Tools: TensorBoard, Grad-CAM for visualization
**** Iterate: Start simple, add complexity
@endmindmap